JavaScript evaluates conditions top to bottom and runs the first block whose condition is truthy. Once one branch runs, the rest of the chain is skipped, and if none match, the else block runs.

code:
const status = 200;
if (status >= 500) console.log('server error');
else if (status === 404) console.log('not found');
else console.log('ok');

real example:
Mapping HTTP status codes to readable failure messages in API tests keeps assertion output meaningful instead of a raw number.

Execution falls through to the next case until a break or the end of the switch is reached, even if the later conditions do not match.

code:
const x = 2;
switch (x) {
  case 1: console.log('one');
  case 2: console.log('two');
  default: console.log('fallthrough');
}

real example:
Forgetting break in a browser-detection switch in shared test setup runs every case after the match, causing double execution and flaky state.

The let keyword creates a fresh binding for each iteration, so callbacks capture each loop value correctly. With var there is one shared binding, so callbacks see the final value.

code:
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);  // 0 1 2
}
for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0);  // 3 3 3
}

real example:
Registering multiple page.waitForEvent callbacks in a loop requires let so each callback checks the correct network request.

break exits the loop immediately, while continue skips the rest of the current iteration and moves to the next one.

code:
for (const n of [1, 2, 3, 4]) {
  if (n === 2) continue;
  if (n === 4) break;
  console.log(n);  // 1 3
}

real example:
Iterating test users you can skip disabled fixtures with continue and stop scanning once the target user is found with break.

A do...while loop always runs its body at least once because the condition is checked after the first iteration.

code:
let i = 0;
do {
  console.log(i);
  i++;
} while (i < 3);

real example:
Accepting at least one click attempt in a UI before checking a condition, such as dismissing a welcome modal, matches the guarantee of running once.

Zero is falsy, so if (count) treats a legitimate zero as an empty or missing value. The block is skipped even though 0 might be a valid state.

code:
const cartCount = 0;
if (cartCount) {
  console.log('has items');
} else {
  console.log('no items');  // runs
}

real example:
Asserting cart behavior with if (count) would skip zero-item scenarios; compare explicitly with count === 0 when zero matters.

It loops forever, which freezes or crashes the test process. The condition must eventually evaluate to false, or you need an explicit break.

code:
// let i = 0;
// while (true) { i++; }  // infinite loop

real example:
Custom wait loops such as waiting for an element to disappear must include a timeout and break, otherwise a missing element hangs the suite.

Use Object.entries() to get an array of [key, value] pairs, then destructure them in a for...of loop.

code:
const config = { retries: 2, timeout: 5000 };
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(config)) {
  console.log(key, value);
}

real example:
Printing or asserting every entry in an API payload before sending it keeps dynamic request data visible in test logs.

In an && chain, evaluation stops at the first falsy value; in an || chain, it stops at the first truthy value. Later branches never run once the result is decided.

code:
const user = login() && fetchProfile();
const fallback = env.RUN || 'local';

real example:
Guarding with element && element.textContent avoids errors on null locator results while making sure the text extraction only runs when the element exists.

A label renames a loop so break and continue can target an outer loop instead of only the innermost one.

code:
outer: for (const a of [1, 2]) {
  for (const b of [1, 2]) {
    if (b === 2) break outer;
    console.log(a, b);
  }
}

real example:
Scanning a matrix of test environments for the first failing combination and stopping all looping is cleaner with a named outer label.

The for loop can use break and continue and control the index. forEach calls a callback for every element and cannot be stopped early except by throwing.

code:
for (const [i, item] of arr.entries()) {
  if (i > 2) break;
}
arr.forEach((item, i) => console.log(i, item));

real example:
Use a for...of loop when scanning locator lists and stopping on the first failing row; forEach would need an exception to stop.

All three clauses are optional, so for (;;) has an always-true condition and loops forever until a break, return, or exception exits it.

code:
let i = 0;
for (;;) {
  i++;
  if (i === 3) break;
}

real example:
Retry helpers sometimes use for (;;) as a deliberate infinite loop guarded by a count limit and break, making the exit logic explicit.
